    <details class="nut-sec" id="SEC_NOTIFY" open>
        <summary>Notifications</summary>
        <div class="nut-sec-body">
        <dl>
            <dt><label for="REPLBATTMSG">Battery Replacement Notification:</label></dt>
            <dd>
                <select class="nut-run" id="REPLBATTMSG" name="REPLBATTMSG" size="1">
                    <?=mk_option($nut_replbattmsg, 'disable', 'No');?>
                    <?=mk_option($nut_replbattmsg, 'enable', 'Yes');?>
                </select>
            </dd>
        </dl>

        <blockquote class="inline_help">
            <p>Set to <strong><em>Yes</em></strong> to receive a notification when the UPS reports the batteries as needing replacement.</p>
            <p>By default this notification will be sent every 12 hours until the batteries are replaced.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <dl>
            <dt><label for="ONBATTDELAY">Only Alert for On Battery After:</label></dt>
            <dd>
                <input id="ONBATTDELAY" type="number" class="narrow nut-run" name="ONBATTDELAY" value="<?=$nut_onbattdelay?>" min="0" max="86400" step="1" required data-err="Enter a whole number from 0 to 86400.">
                <em>seconds (0 = alert immediately)</em>
            </dd>
        </dl>

        <blockquote class="inline_help">
            <p>Short power blips would otherwise notify you every time. With a value above 0 the on-battery alert (system message and notification) is only sent if the UPS is still on battery after this many seconds, and the "on line" alert only follows an alert that was actually sent.</p>
            <p>Shutdown handling and the <strong><em>ONBATT</em></strong> / <strong><em>ONLINE</em></strong> hooks are not delayed.</p>
            <p>While this is above 0 (or the EARLY_STAGE delay below), the plugin manages the <strong><em>CMDSCRIPT</em></strong>, <strong><em>PIPEFN</em></strong> and <strong><em>LOCKFN</em></strong> directives of <strong><em>upssched.conf</em></strong>, a custom <strong><em>upssched.conf</em></strong> directive for any of them is then reported as a conflict when applying.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <dl>
            <dt><label for="EARLYSTAGE">Run the EARLY_STAGE Hook After:</label></dt>
            <dd>
                <input id="EARLYSTAGE" type="number" class="narrow nut-run" name="EARLYSTAGE" value="<?=$nut_earlystage?>" min="0" max="86400" step="1" required data-err="Enter a whole number from 0 to 86400.">
                <em>seconds on battery (0 = off)</em>
            </dd>
        </dl>

        <blockquote class="inline_help">
            <p>Runs your <strong><em>EARLY_STAGE</em></strong> function from <strong><em>/boot/config/plugins/nut3/ups/xnut-notify-hooks.sh</em></strong> once a UPS has been on battery this long, e.g. to stop VMs or reduce the GPU load before the shutdown criteria are met. It is cancelled if power returns first, and runs for every monitored UPS (the UPS is in the <strong><em>UPSNAME</em></strong> variable).</p>
            <p>Hooks you can define in that file: <strong><em>ONBATT</em></strong>, <strong><em>ONLINE</em></strong>, <strong><em>ONBATT_SHUTDOWN</em></strong>, <strong><em>REPLBATT</em></strong>, <strong><em>EARLY_STAGE</em></strong>, and one named after any other upsmon event you enable.</p>
            <p>While this is above 0 (or the on-battery alert delay above), the plugin manages the <strong><em>CMDSCRIPT</em></strong>, <strong><em>PIPEFN</em></strong> and <strong><em>LOCKFN</em></strong> directives of <strong><em>upssched.conf</em></strong>, a custom <strong><em>CMDSCRIPT</em></strong> is then reported as a conflict when applying.</p>
        </blockquote>
        </div>
    </details>
